WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:03.442
(ethereal music)

00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:05.975
- [Narrator] 150 feet underground

00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:08.308
in 30 million year old caves

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:12.850
an unforgettable sight
glitters in the dark.

00:00:19.461 --> 00:00:22.132
The Waitomo Glowworm Caves in New Zealand

00:00:22.132 --> 00:00:23.926
are home to about 30,000

00:00:23.926 --> 00:00:27.259
endemic Arachnocampa luminosa glowworms.

00:00:32.207 --> 00:00:36.961
They spend their days spinning
silk nests from the ceilings

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:39.494
and then lowering
threads to fish for prey,

00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:41.077
much like a spider.

00:00:42.676 --> 00:00:47.178
The luminescent bead-like
strings are spell binding.

00:00:47.178 --> 00:00:49.647
The glowworms use light to attract prey

00:00:49.647 --> 00:00:51.364
and burn off waste.

00:00:51.364 --> 00:00:53.720
The glow is created by a
combination of chemicals

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.427
given off by the glowworm
and the oxygen in the air.

00:00:58.427 --> 00:01:00.476
The caves are ideal for the worms

00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:02.295
as they need a dark, damp location

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.986
where their light can be seen.

00:01:04.986 --> 00:01:06.856
The Waitomo Caves they call home

00:01:06.856 --> 00:01:08.954
are named after the Maori words wai,

00:01:08.954 --> 00:01:10.377
which means water,

00:01:10.377 --> 00:01:13.294
and tomo which means hole or shaft.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.703
And although they live deep underground,

00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:20.870
the journey to see them is
never one to disappoint.

